WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.952
- [Voiceover] Hey fellas,
you doing all that cursing.

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:04.096
But do you even know where
those words came from?

00:00:04.096 --> 00:00:06.965
It's The Origins of Curse Words.

00:00:07.603 --> 00:00:10.162
Fuck. For example, "Oh, a red light.

00:00:10.162 --> 00:00:11.397
"Well, fuck it."

00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:13.213
Fuck is thought to be
in relation to the words

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:15.507
to move back and forth and to strike,

00:00:15.507 --> 00:00:18.582
from Swedish, German and Dutch languages.

00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:21.899
It was used in a coded poem around 1475AD.

00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:24.266
And when decoded, roughly translates to:

00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:26.893
They are not in heaven because
they fuck the wives of Ely.

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:29.553
Historically the first throw of shade.

00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:30.814
And bitch.

00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:32.418
Not something you always called Lassie.

00:00:32.418 --> 00:00:35.268
The direct origins of the
word are questionable.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.195
Likely first used as
an insult against women

00:00:37.195 --> 00:00:39.448
in the 14th and 15th Century.

00:00:39.448 --> 00:00:40.991
Bad move, Billy.

00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:43.577
Where twerking came from... ass.

00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:45.245
With the official definition being

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:47.375
the description of a donkey.

00:00:47.375 --> 00:00:50.334
And that's what we call
from assy to classy.

00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:52.711
How could we forget shit.

00:00:52.711 --> 00:00:54.421
Originally not a bad word at all.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:57.090
"Gee whizz, Becky, your
dribbling sure is shit."

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.146
Coming from Old English, German and Dutch.

00:01:00.884 --> 00:01:03.055
And we can't forget damn.

00:01:03.055 --> 00:01:04.556
For example if Stacey said,

00:01:04.556 --> 00:01:05.706
"Damn, Billy, when you gonna let me

00:01:05.706 --> 00:01:07.142
get a piece of that ass."

00:01:07.142 --> 00:01:08.643
There you go, Stacey.

00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:10.145
Damn comes from a latin word,

00:01:10.145 --> 00:01:12.481
to condemn or to inflict loss on.

00:01:12.481 --> 00:01:15.188
The term is associated with hell.

00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:17.194
And it was popularized
by 18th Century poet,

00:01:17.194 --> 00:01:18.792
Alexander Pope, with the phrase,

00:01:18.792 --> 00:01:20.973
"Damn with faint praise."

00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:23.296
Thanks for joining us, and
we'll see you next time

00:01:23.296 --> 00:01:25.744
on The Origins of Curse Words.

